14:04:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: I'd gently push back on that premise first: Spain is generally known for being warm, sociable, and welcoming—it's one of the more commonly cited "friendly country" stereotypes, actually. Your cousin may be dealing with some outdated or inaccurate info. That said, moving anywhere new comes with an adjustment period, so here's some genuinely useful advice: **Understanding the culture** - Spanish social life often happens in groups and public spaces (bars, plazas, parks) rather than through quick one-on-one small talk. People may seem reserved with strangers initially but warm up fast once there's a connection. - Directness is common—Spaniards often speak frankly, which some newcomers misread as unfriendliness when it's really just a different communication style. - Meal and socializing times run later than many countries (dinner at 9-10pm is normal), which can feel isolating until you adjust your rhythm to match. **Practical tips for connecting** - Learning Spanish (or the regional language, if in Catalonia, Basque Country, etc.) makes an enormous difference—effort is appreciated even if imperfect. - Joining local activities—sports clubs, language exchanges (intercambios), hobby groups—is how most expats build real friendships. - Saying yes to invitations, however small, matters. Relationships often build slowly through repeated casual contact (same café, same neighbors) rather than instant closeness. - Being patient with bureaucracy and slower service pace helps avoid frustration that can color first impressions. **Region matters** Spain is diverse—Madrid, Barcelona, Andalusia, Galicia, etc. all have different social vibes. Where exactly is she moving? I could give more tailored advice.
