14:10:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a debatable claim on its own terms, worth examining apart from Spain entirely. The premise — "how you treat strangers reveals true character" — assumes behavior toward strangers is somehow less culturally mediated than other behavior, more raw and unfiltered. But that's backwards from how most psychology and anthropology actually treat this. How you interact with people you have *no* ongoing relationship with is one of the most heavily scripted, culturally-taught behaviors there is, precisely because there's no personal relationship to fall back on — so societies fill that gap with norms (how much eye contact, how much small talk, how much deference). Different cultures fill it very differently, and none of them are "more natural" or "less coached" than another. Compare: how you treat your *family* under stress, or how you treat someone with zero power over you and zero social script to follow (e.g., a random person after a car crash), might be a better candidate for "character" than how you order coffee — because in the coffee interaction, everyone's basically running a cultural script, and the script varies enormously by country without tracking kindness at all. The reason "cultural context" isn't just "overcomplicating" a simple thing is that this specific behavior — stranger interaction style — is one of the *most* culturally variable behaviors that exists, not one of the least. If it were universal, "context" would indeed be irrelevant noise. But it's precisely the fact that Japanese, American, French, and Spanish stranger-norms all differ so much that tells you this isn't reading pure character — it's reading trained behavior, which is a different thing. You're welcome to still trust your gut read here — plenty of people do, and it's a reasonable heuristic in daily life. But "simple to read" and "accurate" aren't the same claim, and I don't think this one holds up as a general principle once you look at how much stranger-etiquette varies without tracking actual warmth or cruelty underneath it.
